{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat research_journal.md'","aggregated_output":"# Forecasting research journal\n\n## Contract and baseline — 2026-10-06\nRead `/workspace/instruction.md` and `/workspace/task.toml` with tools. Only public calibration targets and public features are used. The frozen scorer protocol is seeds 17, 29, 43, fresh fits and independent checkpoint reloads; lower complete masked MAE wins. `solve.sh` and frozen assets remain untouched. Limit numerical libraries to two threads.\n\nInitial identity method, trusted evaluation `3263a928d9624cfbb0be85c34446d5df`: **15.4218571566 MAE**, score 0, all quality gates passed. Calibration identity MAE is 14.993437; errors grow from 12.6972 at horizon 1 to 17.2183 at horizon 12. There are 74 calibration origins, 12 horizons, 170 sensors; 99.667% of calibration targets are finite and positive. Anchor deviations correlate with calibration residuals (0.163), while recent-history deviations alone do not. Calibration origins are contiguous 12-step blocks across roughly three days.\n\nNext experiment: regularized robust residual regression, sharing dynamic coefficients across sensors and learning shrunken sensor/time-of-day corrections. Check on calibration origin blocks before evaluating the full public protocol. Do not reconstruct development targets from overlapping historical observations.\n\n## Experiment 1 — robust hierarchical residual calibration\nImplemented pooled residual regression followed by sensor-specific deviations with regularization. Features include horizon, daily Fourier terms, base level, anchor deviation, recent-history deviations and trends, and selected interactions. Iterative weighted least squares approximates absolute-error fitting, masks invalid/nonpositive calibration targets, and clamps forecasts to zero. Thread pools are capped at two.\n\nCalibration origin-block validation: last 24 origins **14.563882** versus identity **15.007766**; middle 24 origins **13.801041** versus **14.435219**. Fresh complete protected evaluation `86c1af606caf48f8ad79203a3d515352`: **15.0648913575 MAE**, score **0.7541461191**, all three seeds and reload quality gates pass. Retained: improves identity by **0.3569657991** (2.3147%). Source snapshot retained as `calibration.py` for reuse.\n\nNext discriminating experiment: add a pooled nonlinear residual model to this calibration. Validate by origin blocks to reject corrections that only fit the small calibration period. Features can use local traffic level, anchor disagreement, history trend/dispersion, horizon, and daily clock; no development targets are used.\n\n## Experiment 2 — pooled nonlinear median residual trees\nReplaced the linear calibrator with histogram gradient-boosted trees trained on valid calibration residuals using absolute-error loss. Inputs: base level, anchor disagreement/level, recent history means and deviations, dispersion and trend, horizon, time of day, forecast/anchor horizon derivatives, and categorical sensor identity. Ninety shallow boosting stages, substantial minimum leaf size and regularization constrain fitting; output remains finite and nonnegative. Also tested a tree on linear-model residuals locally: it was weaker than the direct residual tree, so it was not submitted.\n\nCalibration block validation, direct tree: last 24 **13.627767** (linear **14.563882**), middle 24 **13.206431** (linear **13.801041**). Full protected evaluation `2e850e3255814271aedf8c3397fc72d6`: **14.1452638897 MAE**, score **2.6970030751**, all seeds/reloads passed. Retained: improvement over prior best **0.9196274678**, over identity **1.2765932669** (8.2778%). Runnable entry point now uses `tree_calibration.py`; `calibration.py` retains the earlier linear method for experimental comparisons.\n\nNext discriminating experiment: add aggregate network context and multi-horizon summaries. Calibration origin median residuals range approximately -5 to +9, suggesting shared traffic conditions might explain errors beyond each sensor's local history. Test on both origin blocks and then with the unchanged protected protocol if promising.\n\n## Experiment 3 — rejected network context / longer boosting\nTried aggregate network statistics and 180 boosting stages. Context features were neutral-to-worse in origin-block validation (last 24 13.655 versus 13.628 without context; middle 24 13.212 versus 13.206), and the 180-stage complete public run scored **14.1455193190** versus retained 90-stage **14.1452638897**. Rejected both changes; `method.py` is restored to the 90-stage direct tree. Next investigation: only if more budget is available, try graph-neighbor residual summaries with strict block validation; current best remains the direct local-context tree.\n\n## Final retained verification\nRestored the 90-stage direct tree and reran `./solve.sh` under the complete unchanged seed protocol. Final trusted evaluation `0997c81d47a54797b3da31d2bc5913b8`: **14.1452638897 masked MAE**, normalized score **2.6970030751**, seeds 17/29/43, quality gates passed. This is the runnable best method left in `method.py`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
