{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_97","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat research_journal.md'","aggregated_output":"# Forecasting research journal\n\n## Contract and baseline — 2026-10-06\nRead `/workspace/instruction.md` and `/workspace/task.toml` with tools. Only public calibration targets and public features are used. The frozen scorer protocol is seeds 17, 29, 43, fresh fits and independent checkpoint reloads; lower complete masked MAE wins. `solve.sh` and frozen assets remain untouched. Limit numerical libraries to two threads.\n\nInitial identity method, trusted evaluation `3263a928d9624cfbb0be85c34446d5df`: **15.4218571566 MAE**, score 0, all quality gates passed. Calibration identity MAE is 14.993437; errors grow from 12.6972 at horizon 1 to 17.2183 at horizon 12. There are 74 calibration origins, 12 horizons, 170 sensors; 99.667% of calibration targets are finite and positive. Anchor deviations correlate with calibration residuals (0.163), while recent-history deviations alone do not. Calibration origins are contiguous 12-step blocks across roughly three days.\n\nNext experiment: regularized robust residual regression, sharing dynamic coefficients across sensors and learning shrunken sensor/time-of-day corrections. Check on calibration origin blocks before evaluating the full public protocol. Do not reconstruct development targets from overlapping historical observations.\n\n## Experiment 1 — robust hierarchical residual calibration\nImplemented pooled residual regression followed by sensor-specific deviations with regularization. Features include horizon, daily Fourier terms, base level, anchor deviation, recent-history deviations and trends, and selected interactions. Iterative weighted least squares approximates absolute-error fitting, masks invalid/nonpositive calibration targets, and clamps forecasts to zero. Thread pools are capped at two.\n\nCalibration origin-block validation: last 24 origins **14.563882** versus identity **15.007766**; middle 24 origins **13.801041** versus **14.435219**. Fresh complete protected evaluation `86c1af606caf48f8ad79203a3d515352`: **15.0648913575 MAE**, score **0.7541461191**, all three seeds and reload quality gates pass. Retained: improves identity by **0.3569657991** (2.3147%). Source snapshot retained as `calibration.py` for reuse.\n\nNext discriminating experiment: add a pooled nonlinear residual model to this calibration. Validate by origin blocks to reject corrections that only fit the small calibration period. Features can use local traffic level, anchor disagreement, history trend/dispersion, horizon, and daily clock; no development targets are used.\n\n## Experiment 2 — pooled nonlinear median residual trees\nReplaced the linear calibrator with histogram gradient-boosted trees trained on valid calibration residuals using absolute-error loss. Inputs: base level, anchor disagreement/level, recent history means and deviations, dispersion and trend, horizon, time of day, forecast/anchor horizon derivatives, and categorical sensor identity. Ninety shallow boosting stages, substantial minimum leaf size and regularization constrain fitting; output remains finite and nonnegative. Also tested a tree on linear-model residuals locally: it was weaker than the direct residual tree, so it was not submitted.\n\nCalibration block validation, direct tree: last 24 **13.627767** (linear **14.563882**), middle 24 **13.206431** (linear **13.801041**). Full protected evaluation `2e850e3255814271aedf8c3397fc72d6`: **14.1452638897 MAE**, score **2.6970030751**, all seeds/reloads passed. Retained: improvement over prior best **0.9196274678**, over identity **1.2765932669** (8.2778%). Runnable entry point now uses `tree_calibration.py`; `calibration.py` retains the earlier linear method for experimental comparisons.\n\nNext discriminating experiment: add aggregate network context and multi-horizon summaries. Calibration origin median residuals range approximately -5 to +9, suggesting shared traffic conditions might explain errors beyond each sensor's local history. Test on both origin blocks and then with the unchanged protected protocol if promising.\n\n## Experiment 3 — rejected network context / longer boosting\nTried aggregate network statistics and 180 boosting stages. Context features were neutral-to-worse in origin-block validation (last 24 13.655 versus 13.628 without context; middle 24 13.212 versus 13.206), and the 180-stage complete public run scored **14.1455193190** versus retained 90-stage **14.1452638897**. Rejected both changes; `method.py` is restored to the 90-stage direct tree. Next investigation: only if more budget is available, try graph-neighbor residual summaries with strict block validation; current best remains the direct local-context tree.\n\n## Final retained verification\nRestored the 90-stage direct tree and reran `./solve.sh` under the complete unchanged seed protocol. Final trusted evaluation `0997c81d47a54797b3da31d2bc5913b8`: **14.1452638897 masked MAE**, normalized score **2.6970030751**, seeds 17/29/43, quality gates passed. This is the runnable best method left in `method.py`.\n\n## Resumed research - baseline verification\nRead the task files and this journal again. Unchanged retained method evaluation `39b122c311e84d9298e53c94ccdb635d` reproduced **14.1452638897 MAE**, score **2.6970030751**, seeds 17/29/43 and reload gates passed.\n\n## Experiment 4 - sensor neighborhoods learned from historical changes\nImplemented `spatial_calibration.py`: estimate correlations of within-history traffic changes using calibration features only, select eight positively correlated neighbors per sensor, and add scale-normalized neighbor anchor/base disagreement, recent/base disagreement, last/base disagreement, trend and dispersion to the median residual tree. No development observations or labels enter fitting. Local validation uses last origins 50:74 and middle origins 25:49 with all other origins for fitting; the middle split differs slightly from earlier turns, so current paired baselines are reported.\n\nLocal paired MAE, last/middle: retained **13.627767 / 13.315339**, neighbors **13.579012 / 13.297705**. Additional relative-scale features **13.573422 / 13.322746** were mixed and not selected. Complete protected evaluation `9b7a1e7ffa6d49ad94b5448f35c9077c`: **14.1214289791 MAE**, score **2.7473580541**, seeds 17/29/43 and all quality gates passed. Retained, improving previous best by **0.0238349106**.\n\nNext experiment: explicit history lags, within-forecast trajectory summaries and calibration-derived sensor level/scale profiles. Compare on the same two origin blocks before selecting a full public evaluation.\n\n## Experiment 5 - explicit dynamics/profile features (local rejection)\nImplemented `dynamics_calibration.py`. Last/middle MAE: lags **13.607527 / 13.328231**, profile **13.594521 / 13.326066**, both **13.630091 / 13.304988**, robust summaries **13.670597 / 13.296052**, both plus neighbors **13.600676 / 13.305833**. None improved both blocks over the retained neighbor model (**13.579012 / 13.297705**); no full public evaluation was warranted.\n\n## Experiment 6 - sensor-specific residual correction\nImplemented `hierarchical_calibration.py`: after fitting the neighbor median tree, fit regularized sensor-specific intercept, horizon, anchor/base disagreement, recent/base disagreement and centered traffic-level slopes using robust IRLS on remaining calibration residuals. Center is stored from fitting for batch-independent prediction. Sensor intercepts alone and three-block cross-fitted corrections were weaker locally. Selected in-sample affine correction: last/middle **13.569863 / 13.288325** versus neighbor **13.579012 / 13.297705**. Complete evaluation `2288d6c5bc914beeaedbd3389753b630`: **14.1433545829 MAE**, score **2.7010367845**, all seeds/gates pass. Rejected and reverted to neighbor tree **14.1214289791**. Small local gains did not generalize to the longer development period.\n\nNext experiment: normalize residuals by estimated sensor noise or forecast traffic level, and weight training examples by the same scale so absolute loss remains proportional to raw MAE. This tests multiplicative corrections across heteroscedastic sensors.\n\n## Experiment 7 - heteroscedastic residual scaling (local rejection)\nImplemented `scaled_calibration.py` with weighted absolute-error loss on normalized residuals; weights equal the normalization scale, preserving raw MAE. Last/middle: sensor median absolute residual scale **13.773086 / 13.370888**, square-root forecast level **13.672228 / 13.435432**, forecast level **13.788117 / 13.554900**. All worse than retained neighbor tree; rejected without full scoring.\n\nNext experiment: derive a regularized Fourier daily template from calibration anchors, align it to each sample's future anchors and compute recent-history innovations relative to this expected daily pattern. This distinguishes daily traffic trend from contemporaneous deviations without consulting development labels or other samples' histories.\n\n## Experiment 8 - anchor-aligned seasonal innovations\nImplemented `seasonal_calibration.py` with eight-harmonic ridge-smoothed calibration anchor templates, per-sample anchor offset alignment, and history innovation summaries. Last/middle MAE: local innovations **13.559227 / 13.308765**, plus neighbor innovations **13.570034 / 13.332818**, plus short-anchor extrapolation **13.588365 / 13.312023**. The local version has a small average gain but a mixed block result. Complete evaluation `bc4256455f4b4255a911befda3325853`: **14.1209047365 MAE**, score **2.7484655986**, all seeds/reloads pass. Retained by actual metric, though improvement over neighbor-only is just **0.0005242426**.\n\nNext experiment: partially pooled horizon-specific trees for near, middle and long forecasts. This tests different correction response functions while reducing specialist variance through the shared tree.\n\n## Experiment 9 - partially pooled horizon specialists\nImplemented `horizon_calibration.py`: shared neighbor tree plus horizon-stratified median trees, blending corrections equally. Last/middle MAE: two groups pooled blend **13.563333 / 13.261141**, pure two-group specialists **13.601248 / 13.313063**; three-group blend **13.582039 / 13.273343**, pure three-group specialists **13.652307 / 13.349729**. Complete evaluation `3082fe8bae624ee2b876f9c905fd1000`: **14.1101708074 MAE**, score **2.7711427039**, all seeds/reloads pass. Retained, improving seasonal best by **0.0107339292**. Each seed fits one pooled and two specialized trees within the same two-core limit.\n\nNext experiment: a small NumPy residual neural network with sensor embeddings and smooth absolute-error loss, tested alone and blended with the horizon trees. This tests whether a smooth response model contributes information beyond piecewise-constant trees. No extra dependencies are available or downloaded.\n\n## Experiment 10 - smooth residual neural network\nImplemented `neural_calibration.py`: two tanh layers (48/24), six-dimensional sensor embeddings, Adam, smooth absolute-error loss and two-thread NumPy execution. Initial 30-epoch fits took about five seconds per local block. Pure network last/middle **13.850695 / 14.179204**; 25% network + horizon trees **13.551673 / 13.329344**, worse on average than retained horizon trees **13.563333 / 13.261141**. Structural follow-up adds Fourier clock coordinates and a direct linear residual path, fixes clock standardization, and trains for 100 epochs (about 21 seconds per local fit). Pure revised network **13.732116 / 13.893861**; 25% revised network + horizon trees **13.452590 / 13.207139**; 50% blend **13.453297 / 13.305152**. Complete selected 25% blend evaluation `110ddace979f4037be0412f6c41e3886`: **14.0354537227 MAE**, score **2.9289942392**, all three seeds/reloads pass. Retained; improves horizon trees by **0.0747170847**.\n\nNext experiment: origin-level bootstrap averaging of the pooled tree component, preserving horizon specialists and the successful smooth-network blend. This tests variance reduction over the small calibration period. Public development feature quantiles are broadly similar to calibration; no development labels or cross-sample historical target reconstruction were used.\n\n## Experiment 11 - origin-bootstrap averaging (local rejection)\nImplemented `bagged_calibration.py`: three origin-level Poisson bootstrap median trees partially replace the pooled tree component, preserving horizon specialists and the neural blend. Last/middle MAE: 50% replacement **13.462832 / 13.208652**, full replacement **13.477996 / 13.219142**, versus retained **13.452590 / 13.207139**. Worse on both blocks; rejected without full public scoring.\n\n## Experiment 12 - public physical graph context\nImplemented `physical_calibration.py` and optional graph plumbing in the existing fit functions. It reads only public `graph.npz`, stores row-normalized adjacency in fitted state, and uses neighbor disagreement/trend/dispersion. All 170 nodes have physical neighbors (548 directed links). Trees only, last/middle: replace learned graph **13.570624 / 13.270201**; append physical features **13.556782 / 13.266412**, versus current horizon tree component **13.563333 / 13.261141**. Augmented network/tree blend **13.480722 / 13.214439** versus retained **13.452590 / 13.207139**. Rejected physical graph changes locally. Default learned-neighbor calculations remain identical.\n\nNext experiment: cross-fitted stacking. Train a network on residuals from three held-out-origin-block tree forecasts, using those forecasts as the new base feature. At prediction, use full-calibration trees and shrink the learned residual correction. This tests error correction rather than parallel ensembling while preventing tree in-sample residual bias.\n\n## Experiment 13 - cross-fitted smooth correction\nImplemented `stacked_calibration.py`: three contiguous origin-block tree fits produce out-of-fold forecasts; the smooth residual network trains with those as its base, then corrects full-calibration tree predictions. Last/middle MAE by correction strength: 25% **13.430840 / 13.203068**, 50% **13.414443 / 13.306279**, 100% **13.690142 / 13.942144**. Selected quarter-strength correction improves both blocks over the parallel blend **13.452590 / 13.207139**. Local fits took 27-30 seconds; complete scoring pending. Frozen public asset hashes match the manifest; 15 Python files, below the 16-file limit.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
